To **start or carry on banking business** a company must hold a **Reserve Bank licence under Section 22**; banking without one is **prohibited**.

**Discretion:** the Reserve Bank may **grant or refuse** a licence and may attach **conditions**; courts interfere only for **extraneous or unreasonable grounds**. It may weigh **defects found in an inspection (Section 35)**. A refusal leaves the company free to carry on **other business**.

**Section 22(3): the Reserve Bank must be satisfied that**

- the company can **pay present and future depositors in full**;
- its affairs are **not detrimental to depositors**;
- the **general character of the proposed management** is not prejudicial to the public interest or depositors;
- it has **adequate capital structure and earning prospects**;
- the **public interest** will be served;
- the **banking facilities already available** and the **scope for expansion** mean the grant will not prejudice the banking system or **monetary stability and economic growth**;
- any **other condition** needed to protect the public interest or depositors is met.

It may also ask for **capital above the Section 11 minimum**.

**Purpose (OECD):** an environment of **trust, transparency and accountability** that supports long-term investment, financial stability and integrity.

**The six OECD principles (2004):**

1. an **effective corporate governance framework**;
2. **rights of shareholders** and key ownership functions;
3. **equitable treatment** of all shareholders (including **minority and foreign**);
4. the **role of stakeholders**;
5. **disclosure and transparency**;
6. **responsibilities of the board**.

**Why banks are special:** they take **public funds in a fiduciary capacity** and run the **payment system**. The **Basel Committee** (February 2006) stresses **board and senior management** roles (including **independent directors**), **conflicts of interest**, **internal and external auditors** and **controls**, **transparency**, and the role of **supervisors**.

**India:** committees under **Dr A.S. Ganguly**, **Dr R.H. Patil** and **Shri M.S. Verma** led the **Reserve Bank** to adopt **fit and proper** criteria for directors, supported by **due diligence** and a **nomination committee**. **Desirable practice:** not more than **one family member** or close relative on a bank board. The **CEO** must meet the **fit and proper** criteria, and the Reserve Bank may add **criteria for the chairman and CEO**.

**Public sector banks:** the **2006 Act** requires **shareholder directors** to be **fit and proper persons**, with criteria notified by the **Reserve Bank**.
